SPEC = {'lang': 'en', 'slug': 'himachal-tour-from-australia', 'title': 'Himachal Holiday from Australia: a White Christmas in the Himalaya, Spiti in Your Winter — Shimla, Manali and Beyond', 'seo_title': 'Himachal Tour from Australia: Snow at Christmas, Flights & Itinerary', 'seo_desc': 'Planning a Himachal trip from Australia? Snow at Shimla and Manali over your summer holidays, Spiti and Lahaul in your winter, nonstop flights to Delhi, e-Visa basics and sample itineraries.', 'focus': 'himachal tour from australia, manali trip from australia, india snow holiday from australia', 'photo_pick': {'slugs': ['hatu-peak-narkanda', 'shimla-snowfall', 'langza-hikkim-komic', 'manali-ghumne-ki-jagah', 'dharamshala-snowfall', 'tabo-monastery'], 'ids': [('hatu-peak-narkanda:g-1', 'hero', 'Fir forest and snow peaks at Hatu', 'Tall fir trees and distant snow peaks at Hatu Peak above Narkanda'), ('shimla-snowfall:hero-1', 'fig', 'Shimla under fresh snow', "Shimla's hillside houses under fresh snow"), ('hatu-peak-narkanda:g-3', 'g', 'Narkanda in snow', 'Narkanda town under snow'), ('manali-ghumne-ki-jagah:g-4', 'g', 'Solang valley in winter', 'The Solang valley near Manali in winter'), ('manali-ghumne-ki-jagah:g-2', 'g', 'Old Manali in the evening', 'Riverside cafés and guesthouses in Old Manali at dusk'), ('langza-hikkim-komic:g-3', 'g', 'Chau Chau Kang Nilda from Langza', 'The snow peak of Chau Chau Kang Nilda above Langza in Spiti'), ('tabo-monastery:hero-1', 'g', 'Tabo Monastery, Spiti', 'The mud-walled Tabo Monastery in Spiti'), ('dharamshala-snowfall:g-3', 'g', 'The Dhauladhar range', 'Alpenglow on the snowy Dhauladhar above Dharamshala'), ('hatu-peak-narkanda:g-7', 'g', 'Evening from Hatu Peak', 'Evening view over the ranges from Hatu Peak')]}, 'kicker': 'For travellers from Australia · New Zealand', 'tag': 'Seasons flipped: Himalayan snow in your summer, mountain sun in your winter', 'sub': 'Fly to Delhi and drive into the Himalaya: a cold, often snowy Christmas–New Year in Shimla and Manali, or bright, dry mountain days in Spiti and Lahaul while Australia is in winter.', 'chips': [('~12–13 h', 'nonstop Sydney / Melbourne → Delhi'), ('Dec–Jan', 'snow in your summer holidays'), ('Jun–Sep', 'Spiti and Lahaul open'), ('8–12 days', 'ideal trip')], 'flakes': True, 'updated': 'November 2026', 'wa': 'Hello Suzu Travels, we are in Australia and want a Himachal holiday. Dates: ___ , adults: ___ , children: ___', 'cta2': ('#when', 'Which season suits?'), 'answer': "<b>From Australia, fly to Delhi — nonstop from Sydney and Melbourne (about 12–13 hours) or one-stop from Brisbane, Perth and Adelaide — then drive or take a short flight into Himachal.</b> Because the seasons are flipped, your <b>summer holidays (December–January)</b> are Himachal's coldest months: Shimla, Kufri, Narkanda and Manali–Solang are cold and often snowy from late December. In <b>your winter (June–September)</b>, the high valleys of <b>Lahaul and Spiti</b> are open, dry and sunny, while the lower hills get monsoon rain in July–August. Spring (March–May) and autumn (October–November) are mild everywhere. Plan <b>8–12 days</b> to make the long flight worthwhile.", 'quick': [('Fly to', 'Delhi; Air India nonstop from Sydney and Melbourne, Qantas nonstop from Melbourne; many one-stop options.'), ('Dec – Jan', 'Cold; snow likely from late December at Kufri, Narkanda, Solang, Sissu.'), ('Jun – Sep', 'Spiti and Lahaul open; monsoon in the lower hills in July–August.'), ('Visa', 'Indian e-Visa for Australian passport holders; check the official portal.'), ('Trip length', '8 days Shimla + Manali; 12 days with Spiti or Dharamshala.')], 'snow': {'summary': True, 'ids': ['kufri', 'narkanda', 'solang', 'sissu'], 'h2': 'Where is it snowing in Himachal this week?', 'lead': 'The 7-day snow forecast for Himachal\'s snow points. All points on the <a href="/snow-tracker/">live snow tracker</a>.'}, 'sections': [{'id': 'when', 'kicker': 'When to go', 'h2': 'Your summer or your winter? Choosing your Himachal season', 'fig': True, 'ps': ['<b>December–January (your summer holidays):</b> the first half of December is cold and clear; snow usually arrives in the hills from late December, and Christmas–New Year is the busiest week of the year — book early and keep a buffer day for weather. <b>June–September (your winter):</b> the best months to go high — Manali to Sissu, Keylong and over Kunzum to Spiti — with dry, sunny days; July and August bring monsoon rain and landslide delays to the lower hills, so route through Kinnaur or fly into Kullu. <b>October–November and March–May</b> are mild, clear and quieter.'], 'table': (['Season', 'Weather in the hills', 'Best for'], [['Late Nov – Dec', 'Cold; snow from late Dec', 'Snow at Kufri, Narkanda, Solang; Christmas in Shimla'], ['January – February', 'Coldest; most snow', 'Snow play, skiing at Solang and Narkanda'], ['March – May', 'Pleasant, spring flowers', 'Sightseeing, Dharamshala, Tirthan; fewer crowds'], ['June', '15–25 °C; clear', 'Manali, Lahaul and Spiti'], ['July – September', 'Monsoon in the lower hills', 'Spiti and Lahaul (dry rain-shadow)'], ['October – November', 'Clear, crisp', 'Autumn colours, apple harvest, Kinnaur']])}, {'id': 'getting', 'kicker': 'Getting there', 'h2': 'Flights and transfers', 'ps': ['<b>Air India</b> flies nonstop from Sydney and Melbourne to <b>Delhi</b>, and <b>Qantas</b> nonstop from Melbourne; from Brisbane, Perth and Adelaide there are one-stop connections via Singapore, Kuala Lumpur, Bangkok or the Gulf. From Delhi it is about 345 km to Shimla and 540 km to Manali by road — we meet you at the airport with a private car and a hill driver. You can also connect to <b>Shimla</b>, <b>Kullu (Bhuntar)</b> or <b>Dharamshala (Gaggal)</b> by short domestic flights, which are weather-dependent. See <a href="/delhi-to-shimla-for-foreigners/">Delhi to Shimla for foreign visitors</a>.', 'Australian passport holders can usually apply for an Indian <b>e-Visa</b> online before travel. Foreign nationals also need an <b>Inner Line Permit</b> for the stretch of the Kinnaur–Spiti road near the border; we arrange it as part of a Spiti trip. Read <a href="/spiti-valley-for-international-travellers/">Spiti for international travellers</a>.']}], 'gallery': {'h2': 'Himachal, winter and summer', 'lead': 'Snow at Shimla and Narkanda, Solang, Old Manali, the Dhauladhar and the monasteries of Spiti — real photographs, each credited to its photographer.'}, 'spots': {'kicker': 'Ideas', 'h2': 'What to see', 'lead': 'Mix and match by season.', 'items': [('Shimla & Kufri', 'Winter favourite', 'Colonial town and snow. <a href="/kufri-snowfall/">Kufri snow →</a>'), ('Narkanda & Hatu Peak', 'Deeper snow', '<a href="/hatu-peak-narkanda/">Hatu Peak →</a>'), ('Manali & Solang', 'Snow and adventure', '<a href="/solang-valley-guide/">Solang guide →</a>'), ('Sissu & Keylong', 'Beyond the Atal Tunnel', '<a href="/sissu/">Sissu guide →</a>'), ('Spiti monasteries', 'June – September', '<a href="/key-monastery/">Key</a> · <a href="/tabo-monastery/">Tabo</a> · <a href="/langza-hikkim-komic/">Langza</a>'), ('Dharamshala', 'Tibetan culture', '<a href="/dharamshala-for-international-travellers/">Dharamshala for visitors →</a>')]}, 'sections2': [{'id': 'plan', 'kicker': 'Sample plans', 'h2': 'Two sample itineraries', 'ps': ['<b>8 days, Christmas snow:</b> Delhi → Shimla (2 nights; Kufri, Mashobra) → Narkanda (1 night; Hatu Peak) → Manali (3 nights; Solang, Sissu, Old Manali) → Delhi (1 night). <b>12 days, mountain summer (your winter):</b> Delhi → Shimla (1 night) → Sangla or Kalpa (2 nights) → Nako (1 night) → Kaza (3 nights; Key, Kibber, Langza, Dhankar) → over Kunzum to Manali (3 nights) → Delhi.', 'Packing for December–January: a padded jacket, thermals, gloves, cap and waterproof boots. For June–September: layers — warm days, cold nights in Spiti — strong sun protection, and a rain shell for the lower hills.']}], 'ctas': {'h2': 'Trips we plan for travellers from Australia', 'lead': 'Hotels, meals, airport pick-up and a private car with a hill driver — one written quote.', 'items': [('/tours/beautiful-shimla-manali-tour-package-from-delhi/', 'Shimla & Manali from Delhi, 5N/6D', 'From ₹14,499 per person (four sharing the car).', 'Classic'), ('/himachal-tour-packages-for-international-travellers/', 'Himachal for international travellers', 'Routes, seasons and tips.', 'Guide'), ('/destination/spiti-valley-tour-packages/', 'Spiti valley tours', 'June to September.', 'High desert'), ('/himachal-tour-packages-quote/', 'Get a free quote', 'Tell us your dates and family size.', 'Quote')]}, 'quote_h2': 'Planning Himachal from Australia?', 'quote_text': 'Tell us your holiday dates, how many adults and children, and what you would like to see. We plan the route, watch the snow forecast or road openings for your season, and send one written quote with hotels, meals and a private car. Suzu Travels is a travel agent registered with HP Tourism, based in Bilaspur, Himachal Pradesh.', 'faq_h2': 'Himachal from Australia: frequently asked questions', 'faqs': [('Will there be snow in Himachal over Christmas and New Year?', 'Usually from late December at Kufri, Narkanda, Solang and Sissu, though it varies year to year; early December is cold but often dry.'), ('Are there direct flights from Australia to Delhi?', 'Yes — Air India flies nonstop from Sydney and Melbourne, and Qantas nonstop from Melbourne; flight time is about 12–13 hours. Other cities connect with one stop.'), ('Do Australian citizens need a visa for India?', 'Yes; most can apply for an Indian e-Visa online. Check the official Indian visa website before booking.'), ('Is the Australian winter a good time for Himachal?', 'Yes for Lahaul and Spiti, which are open and dry from June to September; the lower hills get monsoon rain in July and August.'), ('How many days do we need?', '8 days for Shimla and Manali; 12 days or more to add Spiti, Kinnaur or Dharamshala.'), ('Do foreigners need a permit for Spiti?', 'Foreign nationals need an Inner Line Permit for the Kinnaur–Spiti border stretch; we arrange it with your trip.')], 'sources': [('Indian Visa Online (official)', 'https://indianvisaonline.gov.in/'), ('MET Norway Locationforecast (CC BY 4.0) — live forecast data', 'https://api.met.no/')]}
